<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Find the study concepts, define mutually exclusive new-user entry, account for
censoring, and fit a pooled adjusted constant-hazard survival model.
</div>

**You need:** [the installed packages and practice resources](book-00-start.html#before-you-start),
[concept discovery](book-04-vocabulary.html), and the preceding tutorial chapters.
Run these sections in order in a fresh `book-work` session. All observations are
public synthetic teaching data; the numerical results are not clinical evidence.

## Research question

Among people beginning celecoxib or diclofenac after a year without either drug,
how does first GI bleeding during the following year differ? We adjust for age.
This is a methods exercise; age adjustment alone does not identify a causal drug effect.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
pair <- book_numeric_pair(resource = "gibleed")
two_conns <- pair$conns
names(two_conns)
```

The helper partitions public GiBleed people into two disjoint sites. It never
splits or downloads a real site's records through an analyst connection.

## Find the drugs and outcome

```{r comparison-discovery}
ce <- ds.omop.concept.search("celecoxib", domain = "Drug", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled
di <- ds.omop.concept.search("diclofenac", domain = "Drug", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled
gi <- ds.omop.concept.search("Gastrointestinal hemorrhage", domain = "Condition", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled
knitr::kable(rbind(ce, di, gi)[c("concept_name", "standard_concept", "concept_class_id")])
ceid <- ce$concept_id[trimws(ce$concept_name) == "celecoxib"]
diid <- di$concept_id[trimws(di$concept_name) == "Diclofenac"]
giid <- gi$concept_id[trimws(gi$concept_name) == "Gastrointestinal hemorrhage"]
```

Select the standard ingredients and standard outcome, then inspect descendants.
These GiBleed study-drug records are already ingredient-coded. The descendant
request includes the seed by default: the celecoxib row below is self-inclusion,
not a more specific product. Zero Maps to rows demonstrate no product mapping
here. In another resource, inspect actual products and mapping direction before
assuming an ingredient set covers them.

```{r comparison-descendants}
ce_children <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(ceid, conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled
knitr::kable(ce_children["concept_name"])
map_rows <- ds.omop.concept.relationships(ceid, relationship_id = "Maps to", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled
print(data.frame(mapping_rows = NROW(map_rows)))
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.prevalence("drug_exposure", top_n = 10, scope = "pooled",
  conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$pooled)
study_sets <- list(celecoxib = omop_concept_set(ceid, descendants = TRUE),
  diclofenac = omop_concept_set(diid, descendants = TRUE),
  bleeding = omop_concept_set(giid, descendants = TRUE))
```

## Define entry and washout

Choose first eligible exposure to either study drug. Require 365 observed days
and no exposure to either during the preceding year. Exclude simultaneous starts
so each person's baseline treatment is unambiguous. Later switching does not
change baseline treatment: this is an initial-treatment follow-up definition.

```{r comparison-population}
ce_event <- omop_event("drug_exposure", study_sets$celecoxib, name = "celecoxib")
di_event <- omop_event("drug_exposure", study_sets$diclofenac, name = "diclofenac")
bleed <- omop_event("condition_occurrence", study_sets$bleeding)
users <- omop_population("users", index = omop_index(omop_any_of(ce_event, di_event),
  select = "first_eligible"), filters = list(omop_observed(before = 365),
  omop_has_not(ce_event, window = omop_window(-365, -1)),
  omop_has_not(di_event, window = omop_window(-365, -1)),
  omop_not(omop_all(omop_has(ce_event, window = omop_window(0, 0)),
    omop_has(di_event, window = omop_window(0, 0))))))
```

## Join baseline variables to censored follow-up

One row represents one person's first eligible episode. Only the joined analysis
frame is published; its dependencies remain private intermediates.

```{r comparison-recipe}
comparison <- omop_recipe(populations = list(users),
  variables = list(age = omop_age(), celecoxib = omop_variable(event = ce_event,
    reducer = omop_reduce("binary", window = omop_window(0, 0)))),
  outputs = list(omop_output("baseline", rows = "users", format = "wide"),
    omop_output("survival", rows = "users", format = "survival", options = list(
      outcomes = list(bleed = bleed), tar = omop_window(1, 365),
      censor = list("observation_end", "death"))),
    omop_output("analysis", rows = "users", format = "joined", options = list(
      from = list(list(output = "baseline"), list(output = "survival")),
      how = "inner", cardinality = "one_to_one"))), publish = "analysis")
cat(grep("censor|follow|survival", recipe_explain(comparison)$text, value = TRUE), sep = "\n")
book_symbols(recipe_execute(comparison, out = c(analysis = "D_comparison"), conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_comparison", datasources = two_conns)
```

The censor list and 365-day window define the estimand. Event = 0 means censored
within that follow-up, not “never bleeds”. Person rows remain private at each site.

## Fit an adjusted survival model with dsBase

An exponential survival model assumes a constant event hazard during follow-up.
Its censored likelihood can be fitted by Poisson regression of the event indicator
with log(person-years) as an offset. The coefficient of celecoxib is a log hazard
ratio against diclofenac, conditional on age and this model. This is not a Cox fit.

```{r comparison-model-frame}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_comparison", years = follow_up_days / 365.25,
  newobj = "D_comparison_model", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
```

The installed dsBase uses one-iteration local starting fits and may print the
known setup convergence warning. Check the final distributed iteration count,
diagnostics and coefficients; an unexplained final failure is not a result.

<!-- expected-glm-warning:start -->
```{r comparison-model}
dsBaseClient::ds.log("D_comparison_model$years", newobj = "log_years", datasources = two_conns)
comparison_fit <- dsBaseClient::ds.glm("event ~ celecoxib + age",
  data = "D_comparison_model", family = "poisson", offset = "log_years", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(comparison_fit$coefficients)
```
<!-- expected-glm-warning:end -->

Synthetic data can show an association even without a real biological mechanism.
Confounding by indication, selection, outcome validity and informative censoring
all need a study design; federating a model does not remove those problems.

## Exercises

**1.** Count observed outcomes and censored episodes across the two sites.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r comparison-events}
knitr::kable(book_counts(dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_comparison$event", datasources = two_conns)))
```

Each person enters at most once. Do not add counts from overlapping drug cohorts.

</details>

**2.** Express the treatment coefficient as a hazard ratio.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r comparison-hr}
exp(comparison_fit$coefficients["celecoxib", "Estimate"])
```

This exponentiates a released coefficient, not a patient value.

</details>

**3.** Confirm the final model reports no site errors and stopped before its limit.

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r comparison-diagnostics}
comparison_fit[c("Nvalid", "Nmissing", "iter", "errorMessage")]
```

Compare the valid sample with the intended episode population. Missing covariates
can change it even when extraction succeeded.

</details>

## Recap

Find standard ingredients and their descendants, make new-user entry mutually
exclusive, declare time at risk and censoring, then pool a model with an explicit
survival assumption. Protected tables and model diagnostics remain part of interpretation.

**Next:** [21. Case study: glucose trajectories](book-21-case-glucose.html).
